{"name":"Smouldering","hex":"#b6321e","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"smouldering","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Burning slowly, producing smoke but no flame."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act by which something smoulders; residual heat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/smouldering"}],"entries":[{"title":"Smouldering","blocks":[{"text":"Smouldering or smoldering is the slow, flameless form of combustion, sustained by the heat evolved when oxygen directly attacks the surface of a condensed-phase fuel. Many solid materials can sustain a smouldering reaction, including coal, cellulose, wood, cotton, tobacco, cannabis, peat, plant litter, humus, synthetic foams, charring polymers including polyurethane foam and some types of dust. Common examples of smouldering phenomena are the initiation of residential fires on upholstered furniture by weak heat sources, and the persistent combustion of biomass behind the flaming front of wildfires."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Embers_01.JPG/330px-Embers_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smouldering"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Papierosa_1_ubt_0069.jpeg/500px-Papierosa_1_ubt_0069.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Papierosa_1_ubt_0069.jpeg/1280px-Papierosa_1_ubt_0069.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Papierosa_1_ubt_0069.jpeg/500px-Papierosa_1_ubt_0069.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A smouldering cigarette.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APapierosa_1_ubt_0069.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thum